name: files
boss: magic
flavor: A blob with no story -- what is it, and what does it hold.
brief: Nothing here trusts a filename. `file` reads the bytes and says what a
brief: blob actually is. `jq` pulls one answer out of a wall of json. `rg`
brief: finds a word across a whole tree of files, fast. `zstd` opens the
brief: compressed data almost everything arrives in. `mandoc` turns a raw
brief: manual page into something you can read. Five small tools, one habit:
brief: look inside before you trust a name or an extension.
tools: file jq rg zstd mandoc
---
q: {a file just showed up named|you found a blob called} @bin@ -- find out what it actually is, with `file`
a: file @bin@
out: gzip compressed data
ref: file
teach: file
hint: file, then the path -- it reads the bytes, not the name
seed: mkdir -p "$(dirname @bin@)"
seed: printf '\037\213\010\000\000\000\000\000\000\003garbagepayloadbytes' > @bin@
---
q: @bin@'s name {suggests one thing -- confirm what it actually is|makes a claim -- check whether that claim holds|hints at something -- verify the real type}, with `file`
a: file @bin@
out: ASCII text
ref: file
teach: file
hint: same command -- file never trusts the extension it was given
seed: mkdir -p "$(dirname @bin@)"
seed: printf 'this file is not what its name claims to be\n' > @bin@
---
q: ``sample.json`` has a ``tools`` array -- {print each entry on its own line|list every value in it, one per line|pull out each item, one line at a time}, with `jq`
a: jq -r '.tools[]' sample.json
out: ^jq$
ref: jq
teach: jq
hint: -r for raw strings, ``.tools[]`` walks the array
seed: printf '{"host":"scanhost","tools":["file","jq","rg","zstd","mandoc"],"count":5}\n' > sample.json
---
q: ``sample.json`` holds a ``count`` field -- {read out its value|find out what number it holds|get just that one value}, with `jq`
a: jq -r '.count' sample.json
out: ^5$
ref: jq
teach: jq
hint: -r again, this time a plain field instead of an array
seed: printf '{"host":"scanhost","tools":["file","jq","rg","zstd","mandoc"],"count":5}\n' > sample.json
---
q: @dir@ might hide the word ``secret`` somewhere -- {find every match and show its line number|track down each hit, with line numbers attached|search the whole tree and report exactly where, line by line}, with `rg`
a: rg -n secret @dir@
out: creds.txt:2:secret=hunter2
ref: rg
teach: rg
hint: rg -n, the word, then the directory
seed: mkdir -p @dir@
seed: printf 'user: admin\npass: hidden\n' > @dir@/notes.txt
seed: printf 'line one\nsecret=hunter2\nline three\n' > @dir@/creds.txt
seed: printf 'nothing here\n' > @dir@/readme.txt
---
q: list only the {filenames|paths} under @dir@ that mention ``secret``, with `rg`
a: rg -l secret @dir@
out: creds.txt
ref: rg
teach: rg
hint: -l instead of -n -- names only, no line numbers or text
seed: mkdir -p @dir@
seed: printf 'user: admin\npass: hidden\n' > @dir@/notes.txt
seed: printf 'line one\nsecret=hunter2\nline three\n' > @dir@/creds.txt
seed: printf 'nothing here\n' > @dir@/readme.txt
---
q: `note.txt.zst` is compressed -- {print what's really inside it to the screen|show its original contents without saving a file|read out the plain text hiding underneath}, with `zstd`
a: zstd -d -c note.txt.zst
out: hello from the stick
ref: zstd
teach: zstd
hint: -d to decompress, -c to write to stdout instead of a file
seed: printf '%s' 'KLUv/SQlKQEAdGhlIG5vdGUgcmVhZHM6IGhlbGxvIGZyb20gdGhlIHN0aWNrCna2YjI=' | base64 -d > note.txt.zst
---
q: {this time the result should land on disk, not the screen|keep the decompressed copy as a real file next to the original|skip stdout this time -- write the plain file out} -- decompress `note.txt.zst`, no ``-c``, with `zstd`
a: zstd -d note.txt.zst
state: exists:note.txt
ref: zstd
teach: zstd
hint: -d alone writes the decompressed file next to the .zst
seed: printf '%s' 'KLUv/SQlKQEAdGhlIG5vdGUgcmVhZHM6IGhlbGxvIGZyb20gdGhlIHN0aWNrCna2YjI=' | base64 -d > note.txt.zst
---
q: `demo.1` is {raw manual source, not yet readable -- turn it into a proper page|unformatted troff source -- make it readable|just roff markup right now -- render it into a real page}, with `mandoc`
a: mandoc demo.1
out: DEMO\(1\)
ref: mandoc
teach: mandoc
hint: mandoc and the path -- no flags needed just to read it
seed: printf '.TH DEMO 1\n.SH NAME\ndemo \\- a stand-in manual page\n.SH DESCRIPTION\nThis is a rendered manual page.\n' > demo.1
